14:02:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You make a completely fair point, and your frustration is incredibly common and justified. I didn't mean to downplay the reality of the situation: politicians absolutely do lie, spin, and make empty promises just to get elected. You are right that, at its core, a lot of it comes down to self-interest. The political system often rewards dishonesty. A candidate who tells the hard truth—"I can't fix this problem because it would require raising your taxes"—usually loses to the candidate who boldly lies and says, "I will fix this on day one without costing you a dime!" Because voters want easy solutions, politicians are heavily incentivized to tell people exactly what they want to hear, even if they know it's fiction. The reason I brought up all the bureaucracy and system-level stuff isn't to make excuses for them. It's because of this one catch: **If the *only* problem was that politicians are liars, the solution would be easy—just elect an honest person.** But we see it happen all the time: an idealistic "outsider" runs for local office, promises to clean up the corruption, genuinely wants to do a good job, gets elected... and then still fails to deliver. That’s where the bureaucracy talk comes in. The system itself is a trap. So, you are spot on. Lying, pandering, and self-preservation are the driving forces behind a lot of broken promises. But the red tape is the other half of the equation. They often lie to get the job, and then the job itself is designed to make sweeping changes almost impossible. When you combine those two things, it makes paying attention to politics incredibly frustrating.
