14:14:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a very understandable perspective. If you look at Western Europe today compared to the news coming out of several African regions, it absolutely looks like one place has moved past conflict while the other is currently steeped in it. But looking closely at the timeline is exactly why historians make that comparison. Europe’s massive conflicts were actually not centuries ago, and the timeline of when countries were formed explains a lot about what is happening in Africa right now. Here are two historical realities that might help explain why this is happening in Africa today, rather than it being an inherent cultural trait: **1. Europe’s violence is much more recent than it feels.** While we often think of knights and medieval kings, the most devastating violence in Europe happened in modern history. World War II—which involved horrific ethnic cleansing, systemic violence, and the deaths of tens of millions of people—ended less than 80 years ago. It is still within living memory. Furthermore, Europe hasn't entirely "moved past it." In the 1990s, the Yugoslav Wars saw brutal ethnic cleansing and "tribal" violence tear through the Balkans. And right now, there is a massive, devastating land war happening in Ukraine. **2. African nations are incredibly young.** This is the most crucial difference. European countries had hundreds of years to fight over their borders, consolidate their resources, and build strong governments. They went through centuries of brutal, violent state-building to reach the stability they enjoy today. By contrast, most African countries only gained independence in the 1960s. The majority of these nations are barely 60 years old. When colonial powers left, they left behind artificially created countries with no established institutions, deep inequalities, and rival groups forced to share the same government. What you are seeing in Africa right now is not a cultural preference for violence; it is the messy, turbulent, and often violent process of state-building. These young countries are currently going through the same struggles over power, borders, and resources that Europe went through—they are just doing it in the 21st century, in front of documentary cameras. **What this means for you:** I completely understand why seeing current conflicts makes you hesitant. It is jarring to see. But recognizing that these conflicts are political and historical—rather than an ingrained "mentality"—means that you don't have to fear the people or the culture. There are many African nations that have successfully navigated these early growing pains and are now incredibly peaceful, stable, and welcoming. Countries like Botswana, Senegal, and Namibia have strong democracies and very low conflict rates. If you choose a stable destination, you will find that the local mentality is focused on peace, hospitality, and building a better future.
