14:27:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I really appreciate you saying that. We don't have to agree on the broader picture for you to have an amazing vacation, and leaning on that strict professional vetting process is exactly the right way to handle it. It is a very smart, practical way to travel that guarantees you can just relax and enjoy the wildlife. That 10-day itinerary is going to give you a trip of a lifetime. Since you are aiming for the dry season (May through October) and want to ensure you are with the absolute best, most highly-vetted operators, here are the next practical steps to make it happen: **1. Use a Specialized Safari Travel Agency** For a trip like this, you don't want to use standard booking sites. You want to use an agency that specializes exclusively in African safaris. They personally vet every lodge, handle all the small bush-plane transfers, and provide 24/7 support. Some highly reputable ones to look into are: * **Go2Africa:** One of the most established and well-reviewed safari planners. * **Yellow Zebra Safaris:** Known for having former safari guides as their booking agents, so they know the camps inside and out. * **Audley Travel:** Excellent for building custom, high-end itineraries. **2. Book Well in Advance** Because Botswana uses a "low volume" model, the camps are very small (often only 6 to 12 tents per camp). Because the dry season is the absolute best time to go, these camps book up incredibly fast. It is highly recommended to start booking **9 to 12 months in advance** to get the best camps and guides. **3. Check Your Passport and Meds** * **Visas:** If you are a U.S., U.K., Canadian, or EU citizen, you generally do not need a visa to enter Botswana for tourism—you just get a stamp at the airport. * **Health:** While Botswana is very safe, the Okavango Delta and Chobe do have mosquitoes. You will want to visit a travel clinic to get a prescription for Malaria pills just to be safe. **4. Start Thinking About Budget** Botswana is a premium destination, and because it includes internal bush flights and all-inclusive luxury lodges, it is generally more expensive than a safari in South Africa or Kenya. Knowing your rough budget early on will help the travel agent pick the exact right tier of lodges for you. What would you like to tackle next? We can talk about what kind of budget to expect, what the actual luxury tents look like, or even what kind of camera gear you might want to bring for the wildlife!
